Chapter 3 opens with a reflection: **AI did not arrive suddenly in 2022.** It arrived in **cycles of hype and disappointment** stretching back seventy years, and a manager who understands that rhythm is far better at judging today's claims than one who assumes the current wave is the first.

It is tempting to treat AI history as background colour. The chapter argues the opposite. AI has already lived through **two full boom-and-bust cycles**, each driven by the same pattern:

1. **capability improves**;
2. **expectations outrun capability**;
3. **funding collapses** when the gap becomes visible;
4. the field **re-emerges only once a genuine technical unlock** closes that gap.

Recognising this pattern is one of the most **transferable skills** the course offers a general manager evaluating any new wave of AI hype, including the current one.
